Both cases of the proof bound the circuit size of an auxiliary polynomial, and reach the permanent
and the Hamiltonian cycle polynomial only through the statement that a circuit for
$`\mathrm{PERM}_M` or $`\mathrm{HC}_M` yields, by substitution, a circuit of comparable size for
that auxiliary polynomial. This chapter sets out that statement: every _clause sum_ (a sum over
bit vectors of a product of local weights) is $`\mathrm{PERM}_M` of a matrix of size
$`M \geq 3q` (when $`2 \neq 0`) and $`\mathrm{HC}_M` of a matrix of size $`M \geq 40q`
(over every field), where $`q` is the number of literals and every entry of the matrix is affine
in one variable. It follows Section E.1 and the beginning of Section E.2 of the paper (pages
31–33) and Section M.2 (page 61) of {repoFile "vp-vnp/paper.pdf"}[`paper.pdf`], and adds the two
Lean theorems that put $`(\mathrm{PERM}_m)` and $`(\mathrm{HC}_m)` in VNP.

The paper's own account of the role of these statements (Section 2.1, page 6): the permanent
“enters once in each case (`lem_fkl_perm` and `lem_circuit`), both times through an explicit form
of Valiant's completeness theorem \[Val79\]: both $`f_{k,\ell}` and $`\Phi` are _clause sums_
(that is, sums over all bit vectors of products of local weights), so a circuit for the permanent
gives, by substitution, circuits of about the same size for them. The Hamiltonian cycle polynomial
has the same property, while no such reduction is known for the determinant.” The polynomial
$`f_{k,\ell}` of the flat case is defined below; $`\Phi` belongs to
{ref "chapter-accelerators"}[Accelerators and the diagonal argument]. The caption of Figure 1
(page 5) says of these two steps that they “are the only steps that use the permanent; they use
the circuit too, but only by substituting into it.”

_Background (not from the paper's proofs)._ That the permanent is complete for VNP over every
field in which $`2 \neq 0`, and the Hamiltonian cycle polynomial over every field, is cited by
the paper (page 1, footnote 1, and page 4: “this is a theorem of Valiant \[Val79\]”; L. G.
Valiant, _Completeness classes in algebra_, STOC 1979). The paper does not use that theorem.
Section E.1 (page 31) says:
“The next proposition replaces Valiant's completeness theorem \[Val79\], for which \[Bür00\] and
\[Bür24, Section 2.7 of the arXiv version\] give the classical constructions.” What is used is
only the explicit projection below, for sums of one special shape, with explicit sizes.

Throughout, `Computes f s` is the circuit model of {ref "chapter-statement"}[The statement to
trust]: the development works with its own copy `Nc.Computes` of `Statement.Computes`, and with
`Nc.genPerm F S`, the permanent of the generic $`S \times S` matrix (Mathlib's
`Matrix.permanent`), and `Nc.genHC F S`, defined by the formula of `Statement.hc`.

# E.1. Sums over bits as permanents

:::theorem "prop_grid" (lean := "Paper.prop_grid, Paper.GridEntry") (parent := "part-overview")
*Proposition E.1 of the paper* (sums over bits as permanents; pages 31–32), quoted: “Let $`R`
be a commutative ring with an element $`\frac12`, let $`v : I \to V` and $`w : I \to K` be
surjections of finite sets, and for every $`n \in I` let $`W_n` be a $`2 \times 2` matrix over
$`R` with entries $`W_n(x, r)`, $`x, r \in \{0,1\}`. Then
$$`\sum_{x \in \{0,1\}^V} \; \sum_{r \in \{0,1\}^K} \; \prod_{n \in I} W_n(x_{v(n)}, r_{w(n)})`
is the permanent of an $`M \times M` matrix, for every $`M \geq 3|I|`, whose entries are
$`0, \pm 1, \pm\frac12` and sums of values $`W_n` with the same $`w(n)`.”

In the Lean statement the set $`I` is the type `N`, the size $`M` is `S`, and $`\frac12` is an
element `h` with `h * 2 = 1`. The condition on the entries is the predicate `GridEntry h w W z`:
$`z` is one of $`0, 1, -1, h, -h`, or there is $`k \in K` such that $`z` lies in the additive
submonoid generated by the values $`W_n(x, r)` with $`w(n) = k`. So a “sum of values” may be
empty or repeat a value. No difference between the printed statement and the Lean statement is
recorded in Appendix Q.3 of the paper, and this blueprint found none.
:::

:::proof "prop_grid"
*The paper's proof* (page 32), summarised. Read each row of a matrix as a linear form in
variables that stand for the columns; the permanent is then the coefficient of the product of all
column variables in the product of all rows. Let $`\sigma` and $`\rho` be permutations of $`I`
whose cycles are the fibres of $`v` and of $`w`. For every $`n \in I` there are three columns,
with variables $`A_n, B_n, C_n`, and three rows:
$$`\tfrac12 (A_n + B_n + A_{\sigma n} - C_n), \qquad A_n + B_n - A_{\sigma n} + C_n, \qquad W_n(1,1) C_n + W_n(0,1) B_n + W_{\rho n}(1,0) C_{\rho n} + W_{\rho n}(0,0) B_{\rho n}.`
The squarefree part of the product of the first two rows is $`A_n B_n + A_{\sigma n} C_n`; the
choice of the first monomial is called $`x = 1` at $`n`, that of the second $`x = 0`. Every
$`A_m` is used exactly once if and only if $`x` is constant on the cycles of $`\sigma`, that is,
a function on $`V`; then $`C_n` (if $`x = 1`) or $`B_n` (if $`x = 0`) is left at $`n`. The third
row takes one of its first two terms ($`r = 1` at $`n`) or one of the last two ($`r = 0`); the
variables that were left are used exactly once if and only if $`r` is constant on the cycles of
$`\rho`, and the product of the coefficients is then $`\prod_n W_n(x, r)`. The division by two is
needed for the first row only. A diagonal of ones pads the matrix to any larger size.

Appendix Q.3 (page 70) lists “the constructions of Propositions E.1 and M.2” among the steps
that “exist only under names of the underlying library and not as theorems with prefix `Paper.`”:
the matrix is built in the library `Nc` (the files
{repoFile "vp-vnp/Nc/PermGrid.lean"}[`Nc/PermGrid.lean`] and
{repoFile "vp-vnp/Nc/PermValiant.lean"}[`Nc/PermValiant.lean`]), and `Paper.prop_grid` restates
the result.
:::

:::definition "clauseSum" (lean := "Paper.clauseSum") (parent := "part-overview")
*Definition of the paper* (clause sums; page 32, unnumbered), quoted: “Let $`V` be a finite set,
whose elements we call bits. A _literal_ is a condition of the form $`x_v = 1` or $`x_v = 0` on a
single bit. A _clause_ consists of a nonempty list of literals and a weight. Let $`K` be a finite
set of clauses such that every bit occurs in at least one literal, and let $`w_j` be the weight of
the clause $`j \in K`. The _clause sum_ is
$$`\sum_{x \in \{0,1\}^V} \; \prod_{j \in K} \begin{cases} w_j & \text{if } x \text{ satisfies all literals of the clause } j, \\ 1 & \text{otherwise.} \end{cases}`
Its number of literals is the total length of the lists of all clauses.”

In Lean a clause $`\kappa \in K` has the list `lits κ : List (V × Bool)` of its literals (a bit
and the value required of it) and the weight `wt κ` in a commutative ring. The two side
conditions of the definition (lists nonempty, every bit occurs) are not part of `clauseSum`; they
are hypotheses `hne` and `hocc` of the theorems that use it. The paper adds: “Clauses of weight 1
may be added, so that the condition that every bit occurs in a literal is no restriction.”
:::

:::corollary "cor_clauses" (lean := "Paper.cor_clauses") (parent := "part-overview")
*Corollary E.2 of the paper* (clause sums as permanents; page 32), quoted: “Let $`2 \neq 0` in
$`\Bbbk`. A clause sum with $`q` literals in all, whose weights are constants or variables, is the
permanent of an $`M \times M` matrix, for every $`M \geq 3q`, with entries that are affine in one
variable each.”

In the Lean statement the weights are polynomials in variables indexed by `ι`, each a variable
`X i` or a constant `C a`, and “affine in one variable” is `Nc.IsAffine p`: $`p` is a constant, or
$`p = a x_i + b` for one variable $`x_i` and constants $`a, b`. The field is any field `F` in
`Type` with `(2 : F) ≠ 0`.
:::

:::proof "cor_clauses" (uses := "clauses_of_complete, gridComplete")
*The paper's proof* (page 32), quoted: “We sum over a bit $`r_j` for every clause $`j`, in which
$`r_j = 0` contributes $`1` and $`r_j = 1` contributes $`w_j - 1` times the indicators of the
literals of the clause, and we put the factor $`w_j - 1` on the first literal. Then we apply
Proposition E.1 with $`I` the set of all literals, with the map $`I \to V` that sends a literal
to its bit, and with the map $`I \to K` that sends it to its clause. A sum of values $`W_n` with
the same clause $`j` is then affine in $`w_j`.”

The identity behind the first sentence is
$`\prod_j \bigl(1 + (w_j - 1)[x \text{ satisfies } j]\bigr) = \sum_{r} \prod_j \bigl((w_j - 1)[x \text{ satisfies } j]\bigr)^{r_j}`.
In Lean the corollary is the instance $`G = \mathrm{PERM}`, $`\theta = 3` of the general
statement {bpref "clauses_of_complete"}[] below, through {bpref "gridComplete"}[].
:::

# M.2. Sums over bits as Hamiltonian cycle polynomials

In characteristic two the permanent is the determinant, and Proposition E.1 needs $`\frac12`.
For that reason the main theorem is stated for $`\mathrm{HC}_m` in every characteristic and for
$`\mathrm{PERM}_m` when $`p \neq 2`, and Appendix M repeats Proposition E.1 for the Hamiltonian
cycle polynomial, without division.

:::theorem "prop_hc_grid" (lean := "Paper.prop_hc_grid") (parent := "part-overview")
*Proposition M.2 of the paper* (sums over bits as Hamiltonian cycle polynomials; page 61),
quoted: “Let $`R` be a commutative ring, and let the surjections $`v : I \to V` and
$`w : I \to K` and the $`2 \times 2` matrices $`W_n` over $`R` be as in Proposition E.1. Then the
sum of that proposition is $`\mathrm{HC}_M` of an $`M \times M` matrix whose entries are $`0`,
$`1` and single values $`W_n(x, r)`, for every $`M \geq 40|I|`.”

In `Paper.prop_hc_grid` the value of $`\mathrm{HC}_M` at a matrix is `Nc.Hc.hcSum M`, the sum
over the permutations $`\sigma` of the index set that form one cycle through all points of
$`\prod_a M_{a, \sigma(a)}`. Appendix Q.3 (page 69): “In Proposition M.2 Lean has
$`\mathrm{HC}_0 = 1`, which matters only for empty $`I`.” (For $`M = 0` the only permutation of
the empty set counts as a cycle through all points, so the Lean statement also covers
$`I = \emptyset`, where the sum of Proposition E.1 is $`1`.)

Table 3 of the paper names a second Lean theorem for this proposition, `Paper.cor_clauses_hc`,
the analogue of Corollary E.2; it is the node {bpref "cor_clauses_hc"}[] below.
:::

:::proof "prop_hc_grid"
*The paper's proof* (page 61), summarised. A directed graph with weighted edges is built whose
Hamiltonian cycles correspond to the pairs $`(x, r)`, the cycle of $`(x, r)` having weight
$`\prod_n W_n(x_{v(n)}, r_{w(n)})`; the matrix is its weighted adjacency matrix. The graph is a
cyclic row of _segments_, each offering a few parallel _options_ (directed paths from one
junction to the next). An _XOR_ between two options is three new vertices $`a, b, c` with edges
in both directions along $`a - b - c`; one option is led through it from $`a` to $`c`, the other
from $`c` to $`a`, so a Hamiltonian cycle takes exactly one of the two. There is a segment with
options $`T, F` for every element of $`V` and of $`K`, and for every $`n \in I` seven further
segments: a _selector_ with an option of weight $`W_n(t)` for each $`t \in \{T, F\}^2`, a _flag_
for each $`t`, an $`x`-check and an $`r`-check. Ten XORs at $`n` force the selected $`t` to be
$`(x_{v(n)}, r_{w(n)})`. The count: “There are $`|V| + |K| + 7|I|` junctions and $`10|I|` XORs,
which makes at most $`39|I|` vertices, and segments with a single option of weight $`1` give
every larger $`M`.”

The construction lives in {repoFile "vp-vnp/Nc/Hc/Segments.lean"}[`Nc/Hc/Segments.lean`] and
{repoFile "vp-vnp/Nc/Hc/Grid.lean"}[`Nc/Hc/Grid.lean`]; as for Proposition E.1, the paper's
Appendix Q.3 says that it exists only under names of the library `Nc`. The paper places it “in
the tradition of Valiant and Malod \[Val79, Mal03\]” and adds that “a completeness proof for the
Hamiltonian cycle polynomial along these lines can be found in \[FPV13\]” (page 61).
:::

# Families that are complete for grid sums

The proof never uses any property of the permanent or of the Hamiltonian cycle polynomial other
than the two propositions above. The paper isolates it in a definition, and the Lean development
proves the lower bounds of both cases for every family with this property.

:::definition "GridComplete" (lean := "Nc.GridComplete") (parent := "part-overview")
*Definition of the paper* (complete for grid sums; page 61, unnumbered), quoted: “We call a
family $`(G_M)` of polynomials over a field $`\Bbbk` in $`M \times M` variables _complete for
grid sums with factor $`\theta`_ if the following holds: whenever in Proposition E.1 the ring
$`R` is a polynomial ring over $`\Bbbk`, with or without the element $`\frac12`, and the entries
of every matrix $`W_n` are affine in one variable, which depends on $`w(n)` only, the sum there
is, for every $`M \geq \theta|I|`, the value of $`G_M` at a matrix whose entries are affine in
one variable each.”

In `Nc.GridComplete F G κ` the factor $`\theta` is the natural number `κ` (the docstring calls it
the blow-up). The ring is `MvPolynomial ι F` for an arbitrary type `ι` of variables. A function
`o : W' → Option ι` assigns to each $`k \in K` (the type `W'`) a variable or none, and the
hypothesis on the entries is that $`W_n(x, r)` lies in the span of $`1` and of the variable
`o (w n)` (of $`1` alone if there is none). The conclusion is a substitution `M` with
`IsAffine (M q)` for every entry and `aeval M (G S)` equal to the sum, for all `S` with
`κ * Fintype.card N ≤ S`.
:::

:::lemma_ "gridComplete" (lean := "Paper.gridComplete_perm, Paper.gridComplete_hc") (parent := "part-overview")
*A sentence of the paper after Proposition M.2* (page 61), quoted: “Thus $`(\mathrm{PERM}_M)` has
this property with factor $`3` if $`2 \neq 0`, and $`(\mathrm{HC}_M)` with factor $`40`.”

These two Lean theorems have no number in the paper; they restate Propositions E.1 and M.2 in
the language of the definition. In the Lean development every use of $`\mathrm{PERM}` and
$`\mathrm{HC}` in the lower bounds of both cases passes through them, either directly or through
Corollary E.2 and its analogue for $`\mathrm{HC}`, which are proved from them. Appendix Q.3 (page
70) says of the route: “Lemma K.3, Theorem M.3 and Theorem N.5 use the general forms for families
that are complete for grid sums, of which Corollary E.2 and the statements about the permanent
are instances.”
:::

:::lemma_ "clauses_of_complete" (lean := "Paper.clauses_of_complete") (parent := "part-overview")
*A sentence of the paper after Proposition M.2* (page 61), quoted: “Corollary E.2, Lemma E.3 and
with it Theorem E.11 hold for every such family, with $`\theta` in place of $`3`.”

This Lean theorem is the first of the three: for a family $`G` that is complete for grid sums
with factor $`c`, a clause sum with $`q` literals whose weights are variables or constants is
`aeval M (G S)` for a substitution `M` with affine entries, for every $`S \geq c\,q`. The other
two are {bpref "fkl_complete"}[] below and {bpref "flat_rate_complete"}[] in
{ref "chapter-flat"}[The flat case].
:::

:::corollary "cor_clauses_hc" (lean := "Paper.cor_clauses_hc") (parent := "part-overview")
*A sentence of the paper after Proposition M.2* (page 61), quoted: “With $`\mathrm{HC}_M` in
place of $`\mathrm{PERM}_M` and $`40q` in place of $`3q`, Corollary E.2 therefore holds in every
characteristic, and its entries are again affine in one variable each.”

Table 3 names this Lean theorem with Proposition M.2. It is stated as an identity
`aeval M (genHC F S) = clauseSum lits wt` for a substitution `M` with affine entries, over every
field `F` in `Type`, and its proof is {bpref "clauses_of_complete"}[] applied to
{bpref "gridComplete"}[the completeness of $`(\mathrm{HC}_M)`]. In this build none of the five
theorems of `Solution` depends on it: the lower bounds for $`\mathrm{HC}` use the two general
statements directly.
:::

# E.2. The network polynomial

:::definition "fkl" (lean := "Nc.CaseI.fkl, Paper.fkl_eq, Paper.fklExact, Paper.rename_fklExact, Paper.fklExact_computes") (parent := "part-overview")
*Definition of the paper* (the network polynomial; Section E.2, page 32, unnumbered), quoted:
“Let $`k \geq 2` and $`\ell \geq 1`, and write $`[k] = \{1, \ldots, k\}`. The _complete network_
on $`k` nodes with $`\ell` bits on every edge is the complete graph on $`[k]` with a string
$`\sigma(ab) \in \{0,1\}^\ell` on every edge $`ab`. Let $`\sigma` run over all such assignments,
and let $`\sigma_a = (\sigma(ab))_{b \neq a}` collect the strings on the edges at the node $`a`.
With a variable $`x_{a,\rho}` for every node $`a` and every $`\rho \in \{0,1\}^{(k-1)\ell}`, the
_network polynomial_ is
$$`f_{k,\ell} = \sum_{\sigma} \prod_{a=1}^{k} x_{a, \sigma_a}.`
The variables $`x_{a,\rho}` with a fixed $`a` form the $`a`th _block_ of variables, and
$`f_{k,\ell}` is linear in each of the $`k` blocks, or set-multilinear in the usual
terminology.” The paper's example: $`f_{3,1} = \sum_{p,q,r \in \{0,1\}} x_{1,pq}\, x_{2,pr}\, x_{3,qr}`
“is, up to the names of the variables, the trilinear form of the multiplication of $`2 \times 2`
matrices.”

The Lean polynomial `fkl F k ℓ` differs from the printed one in its set of variables. Appendix
Q.3 (page 68): “A variable of block $`a` of $`f_{k,\ell}` is indexed by $`k` strings, and the one
at the place $`a` is $`0` in the variables that occur. The circuit sizes are the same, and the
theorems with suffix `_printed` state Lemma E.3 and Propositions E.5 and E.10 for the printed
polynomial.” Precisely: the variables of `fkl` are indexed by `Fin k × Ix k ℓ` with
`Ix k ℓ = Fin k → Fin ℓ → Bool`, so each block has $`2^{k\ell}` variables, of which
$`2^{(k-1)\ell}` occur. `Paper.fklExact F k ℓ` is the polynomial in exactly the variables that
occur (the subtype `Val k ℓ`); `Paper.rename_fklExact` says that renaming its variables gives
`fkl`, and `Paper.fklExact_computes` that the two have circuits of the same sizes
(`Computes (fklExact F k ℓ) s ↔ Computes (fkl F k ℓ) s`). `Paper.fkl_eq` restates the defining sum
and gives the coefficients: the coefficient of $`\prod_a x_{a,\rho_a}` is $`1` if the strings at
the two ends of every edge agree and $`0` otherwise.
:::

The coefficient tensor of $`f_{k,\ell}`, a product of $`\binom{k}{2}` wires of dimension
$`2^\ell`, is what the flat case measures; it is introduced in
{ref "chapter-flat"}[The flat case]. Here only the reduction is needed.

:::lemma_ "lem_fkl_perm_steps" (lean := "Paper.lem_fkl_perm_clauseSum, Paper.fklLits_count") (parent := "part-overview")
*Unnumbered steps of the proof of Lemma E.3* (page 33), quoted from that proof: “The polynomial
$`f_{k,\ell}` is the clause sum over the bits of $`\sigma` in which, for every node $`a` and
every $`\rho`, there is a clause with the weight $`x_{a,\rho}` whose literals say that
$`\sigma_a = \rho`. It has $`k(k-1)\ell\, 2^{(k-1)\ell} \leq M/3` literals”.

`Paper.lem_fkl_perm_clauseSum` is the first sentence for the Lean polynomial `fkl`, with one
clause for each variable that occurs, and `Paper.fklLits_count` bounds the number of literals by
$`k^3 \ell\, 2^{k\ell}`. Neither mentions the permanent; both Lemma E.3 and its general form
{bpref "fkl_complete"}[] use them.
:::

:::lemma_ "lem_fkl_perm" (lean := "Paper.lem_fkl_perm, Paper.lem_fkl_perm_printed") (parent := "part-overview")
*Lemma E.3 of the paper* (the network polynomial as a permanent; page 33), quoted: “Let
$`2 \neq 0` in $`\Bbbk`, and let $`k \geq 2` and $`\ell \geq 1`. If $`\mathrm{PERM}_M` has a
circuit over $`\Bbbk` with $`L` gates and $`M \geq 3k^3 \ell\, 2^{k\ell}`, then $`f_{k,\ell}` has
one with $`5L` gates.”

`Paper.lem_fkl_perm` is this statement for the Lean polynomial `fkl`, and
`Paper.lem_fkl_perm_printed` for the printed polynomial `fklExact` (the quotation of Appendix Q.3
under {bpref "fkl"}[]). Both are for every field `F` in `Type` with `(2 : F) ≠ 0`.
:::

:::proof "lem_fkl_perm"
*The paper's proof* (page 33), quoted: “The polynomial $`f_{k,\ell}` is the clause sum over the
bits of $`\sigma` in which, for every node $`a` and every $`\rho`, there is a clause with the
weight $`x_{a,\rho}` whose literals say that $`\sigma_a = \rho`. It has
$`k(k-1)\ell\, 2^{(k-1)\ell} \leq M/3` literals, so by Corollary E.2 it is the permanent of an
$`M \times M` matrix whose entries are affine in one variable each. We substitute these entries
for the variables of the given circuit, and an affine function of one variable takes five gates.”

Lean specializes {bpref "fkl_complete"}[the general grid-family projection] to the permanent
with completeness factor three. It then applies `Nc.Computes.aeval` (substituting
affine polynomials for the variables of a circuit with $`s` gates gives a circuit with $`5s`
gates).
:::

:::lemma_ "fkl_complete" (lean := "Paper.fkl_complete") (parent := "part-overview")
*An unnumbered step of the proof of Lemma E.3, in the general form announced on page 61* (“Lemma
E.3 … hold\[s\] for every such family, with $`\theta` in place of $`3`”; adapted). If $`(G_M)` is
complete for grid sums with factor $`\theta` and $`M \geq \theta k^3 \ell\, 2^{k\ell}`, with
$`k \geq 2` and $`\ell \geq 1`, then $`f_{k,\ell}` is the value of $`G_M` at a matrix whose
entries are affine in one variable each.

The Lean proofs of the main theorems use this form for $`\mathrm{HC}` (with $`\theta = 40`, in
{bpref "flat_rate_complete"}[]) and, for both polynomials, for approximating circuits
({bpref "flat_rate_approx_complete"}[]); for exact circuits for $`\mathrm{PERM}` they use Lemma
E.3 itself. In the paper's words (proof of Theorem M.3, page 62): “There the factor $`3` becomes
$`40`, and the proofs only used that $`3k^3 \ell\, 2^{k\ell} \leq 2^{2k\ell}` for large values of
the parameters.”
:::

# The two polynomials are in VNP

The separation of the classes needs, besides the lower bound, that the two families lie in VNP.
The paper proves this in one sentence each; the Lean theorems below have no number in the paper.
They are stated with the development's own copies `Nc.Family`, `Nc.VP` and `Nc.VNP` of the
definitions of `Statement.lean` (see {ref "chapter-statement"}[The statement to trust] for those
definitions and {ref "chapter-assembly"}[All fields and approximate computation] for the
corollaries that use the two theorems).

:::theorem "perm_mem_VNP" (lean := "Paper.perm_mem_VNP, Paper.genPerm_eq_ryser, Paper.ryserWit") (parent := "part-overview")
*From the proof of Corollary L.3 of the paper* (page 60), quoted: “By Ryser's formula \[Rys63\],
see also \[Gly10\],
$$`\mathrm{PERM}_m = (-1)^m \sum_{e \in \{0,1\}^m} \; \prod_{j=1}^m (1 - 2e_j) \; \prod_{i=1}^m \sum_{j=1}^m e_j x_{ij}.`
The summand is a polynomial in the $`x_{ij}` and the $`e_j` of degree at most $`3m` that has a
circuit of size polynomial in $`m`, so the family $`(\mathrm{PERM}_m)` is in VNP.”

The Lean witness is `Paper.ryserWit`, the summand
$`\prod_i \sum_j e_j x_{ij} \cdot \prod_j (-1 + 2e_j)`, with $`r(n) = n` summation variables;
`Paper.genPerm_eq_ryser` is Ryser's formula for it, and the proof of `Paper.perm_mem_VNP` shows
the bounds of VP for the witness with the exponent $`a = 700`. The theorem holds for every field
`F` in `Type`, with no condition on the characteristic. Appendix Q.3 (page 66) notes that
Corollary L.3, the only user of this theorem, is not on the route to the theorems of `Solution`.
:::

:::theorem "hc_mem_VNP" (lean := "Paper.hc_mem_VNP") (parent := "part-overview")
*From the proof of Corollary M.4 of the paper* (page 62), quoted: “The family $`(\mathrm{HC}_m)`
is in VNP \[Val79\], as one sees by summing over one-hot codes of the places that the vertices
take on the cycle, with vertex $`1` at place $`1`.”

The Lean witness is `Nc.Hc.hcWit` in {repoFile "vp-vnp/Nc/Hc/Vnp.lean"}[`Nc/Hc/Vnp.lean`]: with
$`r(n) = n^2` summation variables read as an $`n \times n` table of bits, it is the product of a
test that the table is the table of a permutation, of the bit that puts the first vertex at the
first place, and of $`\prod_i \sum_{(p,q)} [\text{place } i \text{ holds } p]\,[\text{place } i+1 \text{ holds } q]\, x_{p,q}`,
the places taken cyclically. The exponent of VP is again $`a = 700`. The theorem holds for
fields in every universe and is used by `Paper.cor_allclasses` (Corollary M.4) and by
`Paper.thm_approx` (Theorem N.5).
:::

